\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces The logical geography of the reduction. The gap implies the susceptibility ceiling H1 (necessary, \fcolorbox {cT!35}{cT!8}{\textcolor {cT}{\textbf  {\scriptsize  T}}}), while a residue floor together with that ceiling implies $m_1^2>0$ (sufficient, \fcolorbox {cT!35}{cT!8}{\textcolor {cT}{\textbf  {\scriptsize  T}}}). H1 alone is not sufficient, because a measure with a thin atom at $\varepsilon \to 0$ satisfies it while remaining gapless. The two faces are \emph  {not} the same object: H1 is the inverse susceptibility $1/\chi $ (an upper bound on $m_1^2$), and the identification $H^*=\text  {H1}$ is a conjecture (\fcolorbox {cC!35}{cC!8}{\textcolor {cC}{\textbf  {\scriptsize  C}}}) now doubted --- the free massive composite $\mathord {:}\varphi ^2\mathord {:}$ has $G''=1/\chi \ne  m_1^2$ --- while the implications are theorems. The free massless field shows why: it satisfies H1 yet has $f_1^2=0$, so no positivity-only route leads from the ceiling to the floor.}}{8}{figure.caption.1}\protected@file@percent }
\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
\newlabel{fig:diamond}{{1}{8}{The logical geography of the reduction. The gap implies the susceptibility ceiling H1 (necessary, \LT ), while a residue floor together with that ceiling implies $m_1^2>0$ (sufficient, \LT ). H1 alone is not sufficient, because a measure with a thin atom at $\varepsilon \to 0$ satisfies it while remaining gapless. The two faces are \emph {not} the same object: H1 is the inverse susceptibility $1/\chi $ (an upper bound on $m_1^2$), and the identification $H^*=\text {H1}$ is a conjecture (\LC ) now doubted --- the free massive composite $\mathord {:}\varphi ^2\mathord {:}$ has $G''=1/\chi \ne m_1^2$ --- while the implications are theorems. The free massless field shows why: it satisfies H1 yet has $f_1^2=0$, so no positivity-only route leads from the ceiling to the floor}{figure.caption.1}{}}
