\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces The one-species reduction. The same inequality appears at two scales: as the UV force-variance bound L3 and as the IR conditioned-variance bound H1. The shapes coincide because $\operatorname  {Var}\ge 0$ can only supply a lower edge, while in $G''=E[H]-\operatorname  {Var}+W_{\mathrm  {full}}$ the variance subtracts. The unification is a conjecture; the dictionaries (Ward, residue--level-gap, $G''=E[H]-\operatorname  {Var}+W_{\mathrm  {full}}$) are theorems, and building the inequality would close L3 and H1 simultaneously.}}{9}{figure.caption.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:scales}{{2}{9}{The one-species reduction. The same inequality appears at two scales: as the UV force-variance bound L3 and as the IR conditioned-variance bound H1. The shapes coincide because $\operatorname {Var}\ge 0$ can only supply a lower edge, while in $G''=E[H]-\operatorname {Var}+W_{\mathrm {full}}$ the variance subtracts. The unification is a conjecture; the dictionaries (Ward, residue--level-gap, $G''=E[H]-\operatorname {Var}+W_{\mathrm {full}}$) are theorems, and building the inequality would close L3 and H1 simultaneously}{figure.caption.2}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces The campaign's final structural image. The gap is reached from two independent, non-overlapping coordinates. In the measure coordinate the atom is a weak-$*$ discontinuity and every finite datum is weak-$*$ continuous, so no positive-measure argument can force it. In the $\beta $ coordinate the gap is the dynamical statement that pure Yang--Mills lies outside the conformal window; the window is real, so no kinematic argument can force it either. Both coordinates terminate at the same invariant.}}{9}{figure.caption.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:twocoord}{{3}{9}{The campaign's final structural image. The gap is reached from two independent, non-overlapping coordinates. In the measure coordinate the atom is a weak-$*$ discontinuity and every finite datum is weak-$*$ continuous, so no positive-measure argument can force it. In the $\beta $ coordinate the gap is the dynamical statement that pure Yang--Mills lies outside the conformal window; the window is real, so no kinematic argument can force it either. Both coordinates terminate at the same invariant}{figure.caption.3}{}}
