\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Left: a Källén--Lehmann measure with an isolated atom of weight $f_1^2$ at $m_1^2$ and a continuum above it; the susceptibility $\chi =\DOTSI \intop \ilimits@ s^{-1}d\nu $ is a residue-weighted harmonic mean, dominated by the lightest residue, not by the lightest mass. Right: the thin-atom counterexample $\nu =w\delta _\varepsilon +(1-w)\delta _{s_2}$ with $w/\varepsilon \le 1/\lambda $. The ceiling H1 is satisfied while $\inf \operatorname  {supp}\nu \to 0$, which is why H1 is necessary but not sufficient.}}{14}{figure.caption.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:measure}{{4}{14}{Left: a Källén--Lehmann measure with an isolated atom of weight $f_1^2$ at $m_1^2$ and a continuum above it; the susceptibility $\chi =\int s^{-1}d\nu $ is a residue-weighted harmonic mean, dominated by the lightest residue, not by the lightest mass. Right: the thin-atom counterexample $\nu =w\delta _\varepsilon +(1-w)\delta _{s_2}$ with $w/\varepsilon \le 1/\lambda $. The ceiling H1 is satisfied while $\inf \operatorname {supp}\nu \to 0$, which is why H1 is necessary but not sufficient}{figure.caption.4}{}}
