\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces Anatomy of the Savvidy cell. The negative mode is the barrier-top artifact of the one-loop effective potential, not a property of the true vacuum at $\varphi =\pm v$, where the curvature is positive. The softening it produces is bounded, $c$-independent, sign-protected at two loops, and $(L,a)$-uniform by one-octave support. What survives is a near-critical margin: the measured defect ratio $D/S$ is $0.94$--$1.00$, sign-fluctuating mode by mode, with the $k\to 0$ mode unmeasured. Establishing that the margin is positive uniformly is H1.}}{24}{figure.caption.11}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:savcell}{{8}{24}{Anatomy of the Savvidy cell. The negative mode is the barrier-top artifact of the one-loop effective potential, not a property of the true vacuum at $\varphi =\pm v$, where the curvature is positive. The softening it produces is bounded, $c$-independent, sign-protected at two loops, and $(L,a)$-uniform by one-octave support. What survives is a near-critical margin: the measured defect ratio $D/S$ is $0.94$--$1.00$, sign-fluctuating mode by mode, with the $k\to 0$ mode unmeasured. Establishing that the margin is positive uniformly is H1}{figure.caption.11}{}}
