\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces The roof, honestly. Two beams exist at the ends (strong coupling and the ultraviolet); the base stands; one strut is missing in the middle. The strut has three equivalent names, depending on the coordinate used to write the susceptibility certificate. It is \emph  {not} an order parameter (zero-temperature pure Yang--Mills is a single phase, so there is nothing to condense), \emph  {not} a topological index (any such index is constant along the connected coupling family), and \emph  {not} a Markov-semigroup functional (homogeneous in the Hessian).}}{32}{figure.caption.12}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:roof}{{9}{32}{The roof, honestly. Two beams exist at the ends (strong coupling and the ultraviolet); the base stands; one strut is missing in the middle. The strut has three equivalent names, depending on the coordinate used to write the susceptibility certificate. It is \emph {not} an order parameter (zero-temperature pure Yang--Mills is a single phase, so there is nothing to condense), \emph {not} a topological index (any such index is constant along the connected coupling family), and \emph {not} a Markov-semigroup functional (homogeneous in the Hessian)}{figure.caption.12}{}}
