\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {11}{\ignorespaces The weak-$*$ dichotomy behind every failed tool. Finite data determine the measure only weak-$*$; the atom is precisely the failure of weak-$*$ continuity. Atomless measures are weak-$*$ dense, so no finite sample can distinguish an atom from a thin bump. A functional that forces the atom must therefore be weak-$*$ discontinuous, and is not finitely computable: it either reads the boundary value (A3) or requires the full limit (H1).}}{49}{figure.caption.20}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:weakstar}{{11}{49}{The weak-$*$ dichotomy behind every failed tool. Finite data determine the measure only weak-$*$; the atom is precisely the failure of weak-$*$ continuity. Atomless measures are weak-$*$ dense, so no finite sample can distinguish an atom from a thin bump. A functional that forces the atom must therefore be weak-$*$ discontinuous, and is not finitely computable: it either reads the boundary value (A3) or requires the full limit (H1)}{figure.caption.20}{}}
