\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {14}{\ignorespaces The two genuine diagnostics of the $\mathrm  {IR}$ atom. Left: the P3 approach law $T(\tau )=-d\log |m-\hat  e_1|/d\tau $ tends to the gap $\Delta $ for an isolated atom and decays as $1/\tau $ for a pure cut; it is robust to a background continuum and to the choice of operator. Right: the finite-size law $\Delta (L)=m_1+c/(m_1L^2)$ for an atom versus $(2\pi /L)^2/m\to 0$ for a cut. Both are reads, not certificates: a single finite volume cannot decide, because every discrete finite-volume spectrum looks exponential, and the discriminating content is the $L\to \infty $ survival of the isolation, which is H1.}}{63}{figure.caption.26}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:diag}{{14}{63}{The two genuine diagnostics of the $\mathrm {IR}$ atom. Left: the P3 approach law $T(\tau )=-d\log |m-\hat e_1|/d\tau $ tends to the gap $\Delta $ for an isolated atom and decays as $1/\tau $ for a pure cut; it is robust to a background continuum and to the choice of operator. Right: the finite-size law $\Delta (L)=m_1+c/(m_1L^2)$ for an atom versus $(2\pi /L)^2/m\to 0$ for a cut. Both are reads, not certificates: a single finite volume cannot decide, because every discrete finite-volume spectrum looks exponential, and the discriminating content is the $L\to \infty $ survival of the isolation, which is H1}{figure.caption.26}{}}
